## caveman

Caveman is a token-saving compression layer activated by SessionStart and UserPromptSubmit hooks installed by yashigatakae. It enforces:
- Output cap rules (no fluff, no apology preambles, no padding summaries)
- Per-session level: `lite | full | ultra | off` (default `full`)
- Switch via `/caveman lite`, `/caveman full`, `/caveman ultra`, `stop caveman`, or `yashigatakae caveman <level>` from the shell

Code, commits, and PRs are written normally — caveman applies only to chat output. Errors are quoted exactly. Multi-step destructive sequences are written normally to avoid misread.

## kintsugi (cross-device continuity)

`yashigatakae handoff` flushes the current session + working tree to the VPS as an encrypted blob. On any other machine running yashigatakae, `yashigatakae resume` pulls it back and restores: the Claude Code session JSONL, the project memory dir, and the working tree (including uncommitted changes).

If local changes diverge from the remote handoff, kintsugi prompts: keep-local, keep-remote, or save-local-as-stash-then-take-remote. The VPS sees only ciphertext + minimal metadata (machine, session id, timestamp) — your code never leaves your control plane unencrypted.

Coding still runs locally. Kintsugi is a relay, not an executor.
